# +Count, +Edges, +Start, +Target, +Bound, -Term
# +Count, +Edges, +Start, +Target, +Bound, -Lengths
#   * <module> The lengths of the walks to a target within a bound, written as a set
#
# A family of the reduction series, a graph family written with sets.
#
# The program writes top (corpora/library/top) where the pseudo-code and the
# argument below write {}, the empty choice of the calculus that still has
# it; doc/proofs/reduction_series_answers.md carries the argument over to
# Definition 14, and the generator is the family's Scala object in
# gezi/contest-corpora.
#
# THE PROGRAM is the definition:
#
#     lengths k v = { is_target v, walk k v }
#     is_target v = 0 at the target, {} elsewhere
#     walk 0 v     = {}
#     walk (p + 1) v = { successor (lengths p u) | u a successor of v }
#
# over the unary numerals: a node is the numeral of its index, let bound once
# as in corpora/contest_merge_graph.pl, and a length is a numeral. The
# program is lengths k s. Its normal form solution set is the set of the
# lengths of the walks from s to the target t of at most k edges: at most
# k + 1 numbers although the walks are exponentially many, and its least is
# the distance from s to t.
#
# THE SNAPSHOT is walk_lengths/6, the layers of a breadth-first expansion
# (layer i is the set of the nodes that end a walk of i edges from s).
# PROOF, with ⟦·⟧, D, S and Th and Lemma 2b of
# gezi/doc/proofs/certificate_meta_lemmas.md.
# - (⊇) For a walk s = v0 -> ... -> vl = t with l =< k: at vi choose walk and
#   the member successor (lengths (k - i - 1) v(i+1)); at vl choose
#   is_target, which reduces to 0. The term reached is l successors around
#   0, the numeral l, pure and normal, in ⟦P⟧ by Lemma 2b.2.
# - (⊆) By Lemma 2b.3 every yield equals a pure term that P reaches by a
#   finite sequence σ. What σ delivers inside the successors it has built is
#   lengths q v, its set, is_target v, a successor set, or {}; {} is removed
#   by no step (a set heads no redex and has no member, and a successor
#   keeps its argument), so a pure term chooses is_target at the target
#   after some walk, and the number of successors around its 0 is the
#   walk's length, at most k since each successor spends one of the bound's
#   predecessors. So S(P) is the set of those numerals (a yield with a normal
#   form contributes only it).
# - THE NEAR MISS bounds the walks by one less than the distance, so no walk
#   reaches t: S(P) = {} and every answer on it, the distance included, is a
#   false positive.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - breadth-first expansion by layers, the shortest path by dynamic
#   programming over the number of edges: R. Bellman, On a routing problem,
#   Quarterly of Applied Mathematics 1958, doi:10.1090/qam/102435; and the
#   shortest path's greedy order: E. W. Dijkstra, A note on two problems in
#   connexion with graphs, Numerische Mathematik 1959, doi:10.1007/BF01386390;
# - tabled evaluation, which keys lengths by the bound and the node, k times
#   the nodes in all, where the definition enumerates the walks: W. Chen,
#   D. S. Warren, Tabled Evaluation with Delaying for General Logic Programs,
#   J. ACM 1996, doi:10.1145/227595.227597.
#
# READINGS AND SIZES: in the points' comment below (THE POINTS).
# LIMITS: the reduction family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#   THE POINTS: path_lengths_point(?Point, ?Shape, ?Size, ?Bound), the bound
#   relative to the distance from the start to the target. Sized by a
#   probe of 2026-09-30 (CPUs 0-7, the whole set under a 10 s limit,
#   the bound twice the distance): tabling(weak_head) reads the ring with
#   chords of 8 in 260 ms and of 10 in 3502 ms and is stopped at 12 and 16,
#   growing with the walks although the table keys, a bound and a node, are
#   k times the nodes; it reads two rings of 8 in 117 ms and of 16 in 616
#   ms and is stopped at 48. weak_head reads the ring with chords of 8 in
#   5870 ms.
#
# The shape is a constructor of schema/graph_shape, two_rings or chord_ring;
# the bound is a constructor of path_lengths/bound, times with its factor or
# one_short.
# Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list of bytes,
# imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
